L001151	\subsection{Further background information on the theoretical analyses}
L001152	
L001153	\subsubsection{The crossover temperature in mean-field and other universality classes}
L001154	\label{sec:crossover-theory}
L001155	
L001156	In analogy to the case of an Ising ferromagnet in finite field, in which ferroically ordered dipoles couple bilinearly to a magnetic field, we can treat the  ordered octupoles in an AM as ferroically interacting pseudospins with conjugate field $\hat{h}=\mu_B\varepsilon_{xy} \mu_0H_z$. To this end, we consider the following Hamiltonian
L001157	
L001158	\begin{equation}
L001159	    \mathcal{H}=-J\sum_{<ij>}\tau_i^z\tau_j^z -\lambda \hat{h} \sum_i\tau_i^z,
L001160	    \label{eq:Hamiltonian}
L001161	\end{equation}
L001162	where $\tau^z_i = \pm 1$ is the Ising variable characterizing the ferrooctupolar degree of freedom and $J>0$ describes the interaction between the octupolar degrees of freedom. In order to get a sense of the conjugate field-induced AM order, we start with a simple mean-field analysis of Eq.~\eqref{eq:Hamiltonian}, while $\lambda$ is a dimensionless measure of the piezomagnetism that is responsible for the combined strain and field effects exploited here.
L001163	We expand the equation of state, derived from the Hamiltonian in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Hamiltonian}, in the vicinity of $T_c^{(0)}$ with $k_BT_c^{(0)}=zJ$ the transition temperature in the absence of strain or magnetic field and $z$ the number of nearest neighbors.
L001164	\begin{align}
L001165	    \Phi=\textnormal{tanh}\left(\frac{k_BT_c^{(0)}\Phi+\lambda \hat{h}}{k_BT}\right)\nonumber \\
L001166	     \approx \frac{1}{T}\left(T_c^{(0)}\Phi+\frac{\lambda}{k_B}\hat{h}-\frac{T_c^{(0)}}{3}\Phi^3\right)
L001167	\end{align}
L001168	where $\Phi=\langle\tau^z\rangle$. This leads to the following cubic equation
L001169	\begin{equation}
L001170	    (T-T_c^{(0)})\Phi+\frac{T_c^{(0)}}{3}\Phi^3=\frac{\lambda}{k_B}\hat{h}.
L001171	    \label{eq:eos_mf}
L001172	\end{equation}
L001173	
L001174	
L001175	
L001176	
L001177	
L001178	with solution
L001179	\begin{equation}
L001180	\Phi=\left(\frac{3\lambda \hat{h}}{k_BT_c^{(0)}}\right)^{1/3}\psi\left(\frac{T-T_c^{(0)}}{\left(\frac{\lambda}{k_B}\hat{h}\right)^{2/3}\left(T_c^{(0)}\right)^{1/3}}\right)
L001181	\end{equation}
L001182	where  $\psi(x)$ is the solution of  $3^{1/3}x \psi+\psi^3=1$.
L001183	The crossover induced by the conjugate field  occurs whenever the argument of the function $\psi(x)$ is of order unity, i.e. for
L001184	\begin{equation}
L001185	    T^*= T_c^{(0)}+T_c^{(0)}\left(\frac{\lambda \lvert \hat{h} \rvert}{k_B T_c^{(0)}}\right)^{2/3},
L001186	    \label{eq:Tstarinmeanfield}
L001187	\end{equation}
L001188	where the absolute value of the conjugate field was introduced to account for negative values of $H_z$ or $\varepsilon_{xy}$.
L001189	It is convenient to rewrite the condition for $T^*$ in dimensionless form, where we inserted our explicit expression for $\hat{h}$:
L001190	\begin{equation}
L001191	    \frac{T^*-T_c^{(0)}}{T_c^{(0)}}=\left(\frac{\lvert\mu_0H_z\varepsilon_{xy}\rvert}{B^*}\right)^{2/3}.
L001192	\end{equation}
L001193	where we introduced the magnetic field scale
L001194	\begin{equation}
L001195	  \mu_BB^*=k_BT_c^{(0)}/\lambda,
L001196	\end{equation}
L001197	i.e., the magnetic field that yields a piezomagnetic energy of order the transition temperature.
L001198	If we use our above values for $\lambda$ and $T_c^{(0)}$ it follows $B^*= 179\,\mathrm{T}$, illustrating that the piezomagnetic coupling, while allowing for symmetry-sensitive effects, is comparatively small.
L001199	
L001200	This mean-field analysis allows for a very natural generalization to non-mean-field behavior that includes critical fluctuations. This is easiest done using the dimensionless temperature scale
L001201	\begin{equation}
L001202	    t\equiv\frac{T-T_c^{(0)}}{T_c^{(0)}}
L001203	\end{equation}
L001204	and a dimensionless conjugate field
L001205	\begin{equation}
L001206	    b\equiv\frac{\lvert\mu_0H_z\varepsilon_{xy}\rvert}{B^*}=\frac{\lambda\mu_B\lvert\mu_0H_z\varepsilon_{xy}\rvert}{k_BT_c^{(0)}}.
L001207	\end{equation}
L001208	Then the generic scaling relation of an Ising order parameter holds,
L001209	\begin{equation}
L001210	    \Phi(t, b) =b^{1/\delta}\Psi\left(tb^{-\frac{1}{\beta\delta}}\right)
L001211	\end{equation}
L001212	with  universal scaling function $\Psi(x)$. The crossover between the $b$-dominated and $t$-dominated behavior occurs when the argument of the scaling function is of order unity, i.e., $T^*=b^{1/(\beta\delta)}$. Hence this corresponds to following expression for the crossover temperature that generalizes Eq.~\eqref{eq:Tstarinmeanfield}
L001213	\begin{equation}
L001214	    \frac{T^*-T_c^{(0)}}{T_c^{(0)}}=\left(\frac{\lvert\mu_0H_z\varepsilon_{xy}\rvert}{B^*}\right)^{1/(\beta \delta)}.
L001215	\end{equation}
L001216	In mean-field theory $\beta =\frac{1}{2}$ and $\delta = 3$, which recovers the above result
L001217	with $1/(\beta\delta)_\textrm{MF} = 2/3$. For the three-dimensional (3D) Ising model $\beta\,\approx\,0.31$ and $\delta\,\approx\,5$, which gives instead a value of $1/(\beta\delta)_\textrm{3D}\,\approx 0.64$. For the two-dimensional (2D) Ising
L001218	model $\beta=1/8$ and $\delta=15$. Then the exponent is $1/(\beta\delta)_\textrm{2D}=8/15\approx 0.533$.
L001219	
L001220	\subsubsection{Strain and field coupling beyond piezomagnetism}
L001221	\label{sec:strain-decomposition}
L001222	
L001223	The equation of state given in Eq.~\eqref{eq:eos_mf} follows from the Landau free-energy expansion~\cite{Steward23,Chakarborty2025,Steward25}
L001224	\begin{equation}
L001225	     f=\frac{k_B}{2}\left(T-T_c^{(0)}\right)\Phi^2+ \frac{u}{4}\Phi^4-\lambda \hat{h}\Phi.
L001226	     \label{eq:free-energy}
L001227	\end{equation}
L001228	with $u=k_BT_c^{(0)}/3$ and $f=F/N$ has been normalized by he number of unit cells $N$.
L001229	
L001230	
L001231	In order to induce a $\varepsilon_{xy}$ strain, we apply a $\sigma_{110}$ stress. The induced $\varepsilon_{110}$ can be decomposed into its irreducible components \cite{Ike18}, i.e., a pure shear strain $\varepsilon_{xy}$ and a fully symmetric strain $\frac{1}{2}(\varepsilon_{xx}+\varepsilon_{yy})$, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:2}\,(a) of the main text. Specifically, the strains are given by
L001232	\begin{equation} \label{eq:decomposition}
L001233	\begin{aligned}
L001234	    \frac{1}{2}(\varepsilon_{xx}+\varepsilon_{yy}) &= \frac{1}{2} (1 - \nu) \varepsilon_{110} \\
L001235	    \varepsilon_{xy} &= \frac{1}{2} (1 + \nu) \varepsilon_{110},
L001236	\end{aligned}
L001237	\end{equation}
L001238	with $\nu\,=\,-\varepsilon_{1\,\bar{1}\,0}/\varepsilon_{1\,1\,0}$ the in-plane Poisson ratio.
L001239	
L001240	Based on symmetry considerations, additional coupling to symmetric strain and higher-order coupling terms to the field are allowed in the free energy. This leads to symmetric strain and quadratic magnetic field dependent changes of $T_c^{(0)}$ in the first term of Eq.~\eqref{eq:free-energy}:
L001241	\begin{equation}
L001242	    T_c^{(0)}\rightarrow T_c=T_c^{(0)}+a_0 \frac{1}{2}(\varepsilon_{xx}+\varepsilon_{yy})+a_2 (\mu_0H_z)^2
L001243	\end{equation}
L001244	
L001245	
L001246	
L001247	
L001248	
L001249	The effect of the additional coupling terms is that the symmetry-preserving $\frac{1}{2}(\varepsilon_{xx}+\varepsilon_{yy})$ strain can change the ordering temperature of an AM linearly, while the transition temperature can shift quadratically in field even at zero strain. For further comparison with experiments it is useful to rewrite this free-energy model in terms of the shear strain $\varepsilon_{xy}$ with the help of Eq.~\eqref{eq:decomposition}. The result is given in Eq.~\eqref{eq:free-energy-expanded} of the main text. Here the proportionality factors between $\varepsilon_{110}$ and the symmetric as well as the shear strain, which depend on $\nu$ and are of the order one, have been absorbed into $a_1$ and $\lambda$, respectively.
L001250	
L001251	\subsubsection{Further details on the simulations of the ECE}
L001252	\label{sec:simulations}
L001253	
L001254	In Fig.~\ref{fig:modelCT}, we show the entropy, $S$, and the heat capacity, $C/T$, at zero strain obtained from our model simulations. The entropy was calculated using Eq.~\eqref{eq:free-energy-expanded} of the main text with prefactors that were chosen such that the high-temperature entropy per spin is ln(6) for the $S\,=\,5/2$ of Mn$^{2+}$ (see blue line). Since we experimentally determine the ECE with respect to the total heat capacity of the system, we next added a phononic entropy, that was based on previous estimates of the phonon contribution in literature \cite{Boo76}. The total entropy is shown by the orange line. The heat capacity, $C/T$, for the model including phononic contributions is shown in the inset. The ratio $\Delta C/C_n$, with $\Delta C$ being the jump size and $C_n$ the value of the heat capacity just above the transition, is in good agreement with the one obtained in experimental heat capacity data on MnF$_2$ \cite{Ike78}. We note that the phononic entropy was kept constant throughout our simulations, i.e., we did not impose a strain dependence.
